# Line endings: LF everywhere, in the index and in the working tree.
#
# Without this, the working tree's line endings depend on each machine's
# `core.autocrlf`, and on Windows (autocrlf=true, the default there) that
# means every checkout is CRLF. The index has always been LF — verified: zero
# committed blobs in this repo contain a CR — so this changes what gets
# checked *out*, not what is stored, and `git add --renormalize` is a content
# no-op here.
#
# The concrete problem it fixes: `packages/schema/schema/catalogus.v1.json` is
# generated by `pnpm build` (packages/schema/scripts/generate-schema-json.mjs),
# which writes LF unconditionally. With autocrlf=true git wanted that file to
# be CRLF, so every single build left it permanently reported as modified
# while being byte-identical to HEAD after normalization. A file that is always
# dirty and never actually changed is a file people learn to skip in
# `git status`, which is how a real change to it gets committed unnoticed.
#
# That file stays committed rather than ignored, deliberately: it is a
# published artifact (packages/schema/package.json's `files` includes
# `schema/`, and every manifest the CLI writes carries a `$schema` modeline
# pointing at it), and `schema-sync.test.ts` exists to catch a `schema.ts`
# edit that was never followed by `pnpm build` — a check that only works
# because a committed copy is capable of going stale.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect binary content and leave it alone. Every one of
# the 238 tracked files is text today; the detection is here so that stays
# true by default rather than by everyone remembering.
* text=auto eol=lf
